{
  "requirement": "Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST jointly give the direction that the requirements on them specify. A skill consists of its `SKILL.md` and the files under its `references/` directory. Each topic MUST be stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations in `requirements.cli.skill_structure`**\n1. The bundled skills jointly give the direction that the requirements on them specify. Each of those requirements carries its own `{impl}` on a `SKILL.md` and its own tests. Under the task rules, that delegated content needs no test here.\n2. A skill consists of its `SKILL.md` and the files under its `references/` directory.\n3. Each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.\n\n**Test: `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics]`**\n- **Skill set.** The test lists `assets/skills` and asserts that the directory names equal `SKILLS`: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. It also asserts that each skill's `SKILL.md` has front matter naming that skill. It reads `Cargo.toml` and asserts that `assets/skills` is published through the Symposium metadata.\n- **Skill composition.** For each skill, the test compares the files on disk (`SKILL.md` plus everything under `references/`) with the files `skill_files` returns. `skill_files` is the list the other tests read through `bundled_skill`. A reference file added on disk but missing from that list fails the test. This enforces the definition of a skill.\n- **Exactly one home.**\n  - For every skill, `assert_home` calls `assert_phrases(home, home_markers(home))`. `assert_text` asserts that the home's combined text, `SKILL.md` plus references, contains each marker phrase.\n  - For every other skill, `assert_home` asserts that its combined text contains none of those phrases.\n  - `home_markers` gives each skill 3 to 12 marker phrases covering its topics. Examples: the routing table and glob and name rules for `habeas`; the review prompt and gate for `habeas-requirements`; judgment outcomes and FIXME for `habeas-evidence`; the inventory recount for `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`; the `--map` and `--dry-run` usage for `habeas-refactor`.\n  - A topic duplicated in a second skill, or missing from its home, makes the test fail. The check covers both the \"in full in the home\" and the \"not in any other skill\" halves.\n\n**Ambiguity and residual risk**\n- The requirement does not define \"topic\". The test fixes the topics through curated marker phrases. A topic restated in different wording in another skill would not be caught.\n- That is an inherent limit of any mechanical check on prose. Examples that are not required behavior do not make it a gap. The test covers all five skills, every file in each skill, and both directions of the exactly-one rule.\n- I found no gap and no {fixme} acknowledgment is needed.",
  "references": "5a5c704ea25b3efceb4b7c4b3d3f998ccd6490c969be6f0fcaaf7011ddc4cf17",
